x86_64-linux  the closure types, and nothing else. A closure has no name the standard obliges anybody to match - [expr.prim.lambda] calls it a unique unnamed type - and the two compilers spell theirs differently: cxx1 writes `3$_0` inside the enclosing function's name where clang writes `UlvE_`. The same difference lambda.nonames records for every case with a lambda in it, and nothing links against these - a call operator is reached through its object and the object never leaves the function that made it. Every other name in this case agrees, which is the half that matters: what the case is about is which scope owns an unqualified name, and the members, the enumerators and the globals it names all agree with clang.
x86_64-windows  the same closure types, spelled the Microsoft way: cxx1 writes ??R$_0@?1??viaThisCapture@S@@QEBAHXZ@QEBAHXZ where clang-msvc writes ??R<lambda_1>@?0??...@QEBA@XZ - a different name for the unnamed type, a different index, and clang encoding the deduced return as @ where cxx1 writes the type. All of that is the divergence CLAUDE.md already records under the lambda work, and none of it crosses an object boundary. Every other name in this case agrees, arm64-darwin agrees on all of them, and what the case is about - which scope owns an unqualified name - agrees everywhere.
arm64-darwin  the same two closure call operators, spelled as Itanium spells them here: __ZZNK1S14viaThisCaptureEvENK3$_0clEv against clang's __ZZNK1S14viaThisCaptureEvENKUlvE_clEv. Identical to the x86_64-linux line above, the two Itanium targets naming a closure the same way as each other and differently from clang. Everything else in the case agrees.
